No Windows-claim text in the registry. No "first-machine store" wording. The `index.html` search UI does not promise that opening the page is the same as `ooda add hello`; it shows the lock line and the `ooda add hello` command. The site copy in `Packages.oot:45` already says "Browser search is not `ooda add`." This is the right rule and the rule is followed.

## 4. Cross-check against audits

| Question | Expected per audits | Site says | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| Does the site say "Windows is supported" anywhere? | No. Per `win_compiler_plan.oot:64-119` (Phases 1-5) and `win_toolchain_survey.oot:99-110`, the toolchain is verified for cross-compile but no .exe has been linked and no Windows-native `oodac` exists. | No. `Use.oot:23` and `Limits.oot:53` say "Mac, Windows, and Linux ARM are not this install." | honest |
| Does the site say "first-machine store" anywhere referring to the registry? | No. Per ROADMAP §5 (open-honesty row), the registry is a search surface, not a path. Per `Packages.oot:44-45`, the human page is "not a second install path" and "Browser search is not `ooda add`." | No. The `index.html` and `registry/index.html` both link the catalog; neither calls the registry "the install." | honest |
| Does the site promise NSIS, MSI, or any specific Windows installer? | No. Per `win_installer_options.oot` (referenced from `win_compiler_plan.oot:14,118,144-145`), Phase 5 picks NSIS for v1 but is not built. | No. The site has no Windows-installer text at all. | honest |
| Does the site mention `ooda install` working on Windows? | No. Per `win_compiler_plan.oot:100-110`, Phase 4 (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\openooda\` layout) is not started; the install script at `install:49-54` rejects non-Linux. | No. `Options.oot:48-49` and `Use.oot:56` describe `ooda install` as the Linux checkout copy. | honest |
| Does the site claim `ooda run` / emit-c is portable to Windows? | No. Per `win_c_emit_compat.oot:0-12`, the C emit is 95% portable, chs_rt is the blocker, no .exe has been linked. | The site says "host gcc turns that C into a native binary" (`Limits.oot:10`) and "host gcc" (`index.html:256`). It does not say "any host gcc." Honest on a Linux user. A Windows reader could over-read it, but no Windows reader is the target today. | honest with note |
| Does the site claim the door install works without `git` and `gcc`? | Per `install:49-58`, the script dies without them. | `Use.oot:17` says "You need Linux on x86_64, `git`, and host `gcc`." | honest |
| Does the site claim Landlock works on Windows? | No. Per `win_chs_rt_posix_audit.oot:122-132,64`, Landlock is Linux-only; the shim wraps it with Job Objects + restricted token. | `Safety.oot:132` says "Landlock is a real Linux jail." | honest |
| Does the site claim `setrlimit` works on Windows? | No. Per `win_chs_rt_posix_audit.oot:60-63,124-126`, the Windows shim maps it to Job Objects (memory, CPU) and degrades RLIMIT_NOFILE to ignore. | `Safety.oot:147` says "`setrlimit` also sits under `&SysCap`." No Windows claim. | honest |

