Latent under-claims (not Windows-specific, but worth flagging for Phase 1+):

- `index.html:248-253` lists XDG paths (`~/.local/...`) without a "Linux only" line. Phase 4 will move these to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\openooda\` on Windows. A one-line `Linux only. Windows layout is in the pipeline.` would close it.
- `Safety.oot:137` `import "std/sec/landlock.oo"` does not say "Linux only" next to the import. The token table at line 63 says "Landlock jail" without a gate. A Windows user reading `Safety.oot` and trying to call `landlock_restrict` would hit the chs_rt shim's `Err` per `win_chs_rt_posix_audit.oot:122-132`. The site is silent, which is honest today because the chs_rt refuses. When Phase 2 ships, this row should get a Windows cell that says "Job Object + restricted token."
- `index.html:209` shows the install command with no platform line. The disclaimer is one `Start` link away. A one-line `Linux x86_64. Other OSes: see Start.` would close it.

## 6. Specific recommended changes

All changes are in the door (`openOODA.github.io/index.html`) and are zero-cost text. They are recommended, not blocking. The site is honest today; these are the next-step tightenings.

1. **Add a one-line platform caveat to the install block** at `index.html:209` (next to the `curl ... | bash` line). Suggested text:
   > `Linux x86_64. Mac, Windows, and Linux ARM are not this install. See Start.`
   This mirrors `Use.oot:23` and `Limits.oot:53`. A reader who never clicks `Start` now sees the caveat where the install is.

2. **Add a "Linux" gate to the Files list** at `index.html:248-253` (the XDG paths). Suggested:
   > `On Linux: ~/.local/bin/ooda, ~/.local/bin/oodac, ~/.local/share/ooda/std/, ~/.local/share/ooda/runtime/, ~/.local/share/ooda/share/fixtures/hello.oo, ~/.config/ooda/env.`
   This avoids a Windows user reading XDG paths as their future layout. Phase 4's `%LOCALAPPDATA%\openooda\bin\ooda.exe` is the Windows shape per `win_compiler_plan.oot:100-110`; do not promise it on the door until Phase 4 ships.

3. **Add a "Linux" gate to the `&SysCap` row in `guide/Safety.oot:63`**. The token table currently says "Landlock jail and `setrlimit`" with no OS gate. The same table is referenced from `Options.oot:166`. The page already has "Landlock is a real Linux jail" at line 132; the table should match.

No other changes recommended. The site copy is correct.

## 7. What was not checked

- The `pay.openooda.org/tip` worker and `collect.openooda.org/v1/emails` worker were not opened. They are out of lane (not the door, not the guide, not the registry). The cross-check on `index.html:261,329` and `Pay.oot:23` was text-only.
- The `openOODA.github.io/research/` archive (49 RP-* papers) was not line-by-line read. The grep returned research-context mentions of "Windows" only (`RP-3-1:62`, `RP-4-3-3:25`, `RP-4-1-4:17`, `RP-4-1-3:11,15`, `RP-6-2:25`, `RP-ES-6:9,23,31`, `RP-5-6:9`, `RP-ES-3:5`). These are research-text mentions of "Windows," "platforms," and "cross-platform" in the design-draft sense. Per `guide/Research.oot:11-12`, the archive is draft and "not the install." No product claim is made. Honest by archive rule.
- The `wiki/` repo was not opened. The README at `openOODA.github.io/README.oot:4` says the wiki is a different repo, not the user wiki. The user-facing surface is `openOODA.github.io/`.
- The `ooda/`, `ooda/oodac/`, `ooda/runtime/`, `ooda/cli/`, `ooda/install/` trees were not opened. The audit pulls findings from the four `audit/win_*.oot` reports only; this lane does not read the compiler or runtime.
- The installer decision (NSIS vs MSIX vs MSI vs zip) was not re-litigated here. `win_installer_options.oot` owns that, and the site is correctly silent until Phase 5 picks a winner.
- Determinism of the install line (`curl -fsSL ... | bash`) was not tested. Out of lane.
- The `og:image` asset at `og.png` was not opened; it is the social-card image. Out of lane.
